      "source": "lecture11-nonnegative",
      "statement": "If \\(a_n\\ge0\\) for every \\(n\\), then \\(\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\) converges if and only if its partial sums are bounded above.",
      "idea": "Nonnegative terms make the partial sums nondecreasing. Apply monotone convergence.",
      "proof": "<p>Let \\(s_m=\\sum_{n=1}^{m}a_n\\). Since \\(s_{m+1}-s_m=a_{m+1}\\ge0\\), the sequence \\(\\{s_m\\}\\) is nondecreasing. If it is bounded above, the Monotone Convergence Theorem implies that it converges, hence the series converges.</p><p>Conversely, if the series converges, its partial sums form a convergent sequence and are therefore bounded, in particular bounded above.</p>"
