      "proof": "<p>Let \\(s_m=\\sum_{n=1}^{m}(-1)^n a_n\\). For \\(k\\ge1\\),</p>\\[\\begin{aligned}\ns_{2k+2}-s_{2k}&=a_{2k+2}-a_{2k+1}\\le0,\\\\\ns_{2k}&=-a_1+\\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}(a_{2j}-a_{2j+1})+a_{2k}\\ge-a_1.\n\\end{aligned}\\]<p>Thus the even partial sums are nonincreasing and bounded below, so \\(s_{2k}\\to s\\) for some \\(s\\in\\mathbb R\\). Moreover,</p>\\[|s_{2k+1}-s|=|s_{2k}-a_{2k+1}-s|\\le|s_{2k}-s|+a_{2k+1}\\longrightarrow0.\\]<p>Hence \\(s_{2k+1}\\to s\\) as well. Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(K\\) such that both \\(|s_{2k}-s|&lt;\\varepsilon\\) and \\(|s_{2k+1}-s|&lt;\\varepsilon\\) hold for \\(k\\ge K\\). Every \\(m\\ge2K+1\\) is one of these even or odd indices, so \\(|s_m-s|&lt;\\varepsilon\\). Therefore \\(s_m\\to s\\), proving convergence of the series.</p>",
