      "source": "lecture13-uniqueness",
      "statement": "If \\(c\\) is a cluster point of \\(S\\) and \\(f:S\\to\\mathbb R\\) has a finite limit at \\(c\\), that limit is unique.",
      "idea": "Choose a point in the common punctured neighborhood and split the distance between the two proposed limits through \\(f(x)\\).",
      "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L_1\\) and \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L_2\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta_1,\\delta_2>0\\) so that, for \\(i=1,2\\),</p>\\[x\\in S,\\quad 0&lt;|x-c|&lt;\\delta_i\\implies |f(x)-L_i|&lt;\\frac\\varepsilon2.\\]<p>Since \\(c\\) is a cluster point of \\(S\\), there exists \\(x_0\\in S\\) with \\(0&lt;|x_0-c|&lt;\\min\\{\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\}\\). Hence</p>\\[|L_1-L_2|\\le |L_1-f(x_0)|+|f(x_0)-L_2|&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]<p>This holds for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), so \\(L_1=L_2\\).</p>"
