      "source": "lecture13-sequential",
      "statement": "Let \\(c\\) be a cluster point of \\(S\\), \\(f:S\\to\\mathbb R\\), and \\(L\\in\\mathbb R\\). Then \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\) if and only if every sequence \\(x_n\\in S\\setminus\\{c\\}\\) with \\(x_n\\to c\\) satisfies \\(f(x_n)\\to L\\).",
      "idea": "Forward: \\(\\varepsilon\\) determines \\(\\delta\\), and sequence convergence determines \\(N\\). Reverse: a failed limit supplies points within \\(1/n\\) whose outputs stay at least \\(\\varepsilon_0\\) from \\(L\\).",
      "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\), and let \\(x_n\\in S\\setminus\\{c\\}\\) satisfy \\(x_n\\to c\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta>0\\) from the function-limit definition. Choose \\(N\\) so that \\(|x_n-c|&lt;\\delta\\) for every \\(n\\ge N\\). Since \\(x_n\\ne c\\), the limit estimate gives \\(|f(x_n)-L|&lt;\\varepsilon\\) for \\(n\\ge N\\). Thus \\(f(x_n)\\to L\\).</p><p>Conversely, suppose every such sequence satisfies \\(f(x_n)\\to L\\). If the function-limit condition for \\(L\\) failed, there would exist \\(\\varepsilon_0>0\\) such that for every \\(\\delta>0\\), some \\(x\\in S\\) satisfies \\(0&lt;|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\) and \\(|f(x)-L|\\ge\\varepsilon_0\\). Taking \\(\\delta=1/n\\), choose \\(x_n\\in S\\) with</p>\\[0&lt;|x_n-c|&lt;\\frac1n,\\qquad |f(x_n)-L|\\ge\\varepsilon_0.\\]<p>The first estimate implies \\(x_n\\to c\\), and \\(x_n\\ne c\\). The hypothesis gives \\(f(x_n)\\to L\\), contradicting the second estimate. Therefore \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\).</p>"
