      "source": "lecture14-order",
      "statement": "Let \\(c\\) be a cluster point of \\(S\\), and let \\(f,g,h:S\\to\\mathbb R\\). If \\(f(x)\\le g(x)\\) for all \\(x\\in S\\) in some punctured neighborhood of \\(c\\), and \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=A\\), \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}g(x)=B\\), then \\(A\\le B\\). If \\(f(x)\\le h(x)\\le g(x)\\) in such a neighborhood and \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}g(x)=L\\), then \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}h(x)=L\\).",
      "idea": "Test along domain sequences approaching \\(c\\), and apply order preservation or squeeze for sequences.",
      "proof": "<p>For order preservation, choose \\(x_n\\in S\\setminus\\{c\\}\\) with \\(x_n\\to c\\), using the sequential characterization of cluster points. The sequential characterization of function limits gives \\(f(x_n)\\to A\\) and \\(g(x_n)\\to B\\). Since \\(x_n\\to c\\), eventually \\(f(x_n)\\le g(x_n)\\). Order preservation for sequences yields \\(A\\le B\\).</p><p>For squeeze, let \\(x_n\\in S\\setminus\\{c\\}\\) be any sequence with \\(x_n\\to c\\). Eventually</p>\\[f(x_n)\\le h(x_n)\\le g(x_n),\\qquad f(x_n)\\to L,\\quad g(x_n)\\to L.\\]<p>The squeeze theorem for sequences gives \\(h(x_n)\\to L\\). Since the sequence was arbitrary, the sequential characterization implies \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}h(x)=L\\).</p>"
