      "source": "lecture14-two-sided",
      "statement": "Let \\(f:S\\to\\mathbb R\\), and suppose \\(c\\) is a cluster point of both \\(S\\cap(-\\infty,c)\\) and \\(S\\cap(c,\\infty)\\). Then \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\) if and only if \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^-}f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c^+}f(x)=L\\).",
      "idea": "Forward: restrict the estimate to each side. Reverse: take the smaller of the two one-sided thresholds and split into \\(x&lt;c\\) and \\(x>c\\).",
      "proof": "<p>The point \\(c\\) is a cluster point of \\(S\\): every punctured neighborhood contains a point of \\(S\\cap(-\\infty,c)\\subseteq S\\).</p><p>Suppose \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta>0\\) from the limit definition. If \\(x\\in S\\) and \\(c-\\delta&lt;x&lt;c\\), then \\(0&lt;|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\), so \\(|f(x)-L|&lt;\\varepsilon\\). The same argument applies when \\(c&lt;x&lt;c+\\delta\\). Thus both one-sided limits equal \\(L\\).</p><p>Conversely, suppose both one-sided limits equal \\(L\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta_-,\\delta_+>0\\) for the left and right limit estimates, respectively. Set \\(\\delta=\\min\\{\\delta_-,\\delta_+\\}\\). For \\(x\\in S\\) with \\(0&lt;|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\), either</p>\\[c-\\delta_-&lt;x&lt;c\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c&lt;x&lt;c+\\delta_+.\\]<p>The corresponding one-sided estimate gives \\(|f(x)-L|&lt;\\varepsilon\\). Hence \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\).</p>"
