      "source": "pset6",
      "sourceLabel": "Pset 6, Problem 7",
      "statement": "Let \\(c\\) be a cluster point of \\(S\\), and let \\(f:S\\to\\mathbb R\\). A finite limit at \\(c\\) exists if and only if, for every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is \\(\\delta>0\\) such that \\(|f(x)-f(y)|&lt;\\varepsilon\\) whenever \\(x,y\\in S\\), \\(0&lt;|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\), and \\(0&lt;|y-c|&lt;\\delta\\).",
      "idea": "A reference sequence approaching \\(c\\) has Cauchy outputs and hence a limit \\(L\\). Compare every other approaching sequence to it to obtain the same output limit.",
      "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta>0\\) so that \\(x\\in S\\) and \\(0&lt;|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\) imply \\(|f(x)-L|&lt;\\varepsilon/2\\). For any two such points \\(x,y\\),</p>\\[|f(x)-f(y)|\\le |f(x)-L|+|f(y)-L|&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]<p>Conversely, suppose the stated Cauchy condition holds. Since \\(c\\) is a cluster point, choose \\(z_n\\in S\\) with \\(0&lt;|z_n-c|&lt;1/n\\), so \\(z_n\\to c\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta>0\\) from the condition and \\(N\\) with \\(1/N&lt;\\delta\\). For \\(m,n\\ge N\\), both \\(z_m,z_n\\) lie in the punctured \\(\\delta\\)-neighborhood, so \\(|f(z_m)-f(z_n)|&lt;\\varepsilon\\). Thus \\(\\{f(z_n)\\}\\) is Cauchy and converges to some \\(L\\in\\mathbb R\\).</p><p>Let \\(x_n\\in S\\setminus\\{c\\}\\) be any sequence with \\(x_n\\to c\\). Given \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), choose \\(\\delta>0\\) from the Cauchy condition with tolerance \\(\\varepsilon/2\\). Since \\(x_n\\to c\\), \\(z_n\\to c\\), and \\(f(z_n)\\to L\\), choose a common \\(N\\) so that, for \\(n\\ge N\\),</p>\\[0&lt;|x_n-c|&lt;\\delta,\\qquad 0&lt;|z_n-c|&lt;\\delta,\\qquad |f(z_n)-L|&lt;\\frac\\varepsilon2.\\]<p>Consequently</p>\\[|f(x_n)-L|\\le |f(x_n)-f(z_n)|+|f(z_n)-L|&lt;\\frac\\varepsilon2+\\frac\\varepsilon2=\\varepsilon.\\]<p>Hence \\(f(x_n)\\to L\\) for every approaching domain sequence. The sequential characterization gives \\(\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)=L\\).</p>"
